WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.889
Merge sort is divide-and-conquer

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.507
applied to sorting.

00:00:04.507 --> 00:00:07.258
Each subproblem sorts a subarray

00:00:07.258 --> 00:00:08.471
A[p..r].

00:00:08.471 --> 00:00:11.950
If the subarray has more than one element

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.620
divide it at the middle point q.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.828
Conquer by sorting the two

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.258
halves recursively

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.474
and combine by merging the two

00:00:20.545 --> 00:00:22.189
sorted halves into one.

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:25.406
A subarray of one element is already sorted

00:00:25.406 --> 00:00:27.050
that is the base case.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.859
For example

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:33.358
the ten letters A L G O R I T H M S.

00:00:33.358 --> 00:00:37.857
Divide them into two halves of five.

00:00:37.857 --> 00:00:43.207
Sort each half recursively, and merge them

00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:45.760
A G H I L M O R S T.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.740
All the real work happens in merge.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.670
Dividing just computes a middle index.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.290
How do we merge two sorted runs efficiently?

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.192
Think of two piles of cards, each sorted

00:00:58.192 --> 00:01:00.334
with the smallest card on top.

00:01:00.334 --> 00:01:02.199
Compare the two top cards

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:03.719
take the smaller one

00:01:03.719 --> 00:01:05.515
and put it on the output.

00:01:05.515 --> 00:01:07.726
Repeat until one pile is empty

00:01:07.795 --> 00:01:09.868
then put the rest of the other

00:01:09.937 --> 00:01:11.250
pile on the output.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.141
Two against one: take the one

00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:15.496
from the right.

00:01:15.496 --> 00:01:18.291
Two against two: they are equal

00:01:18.291 --> 00:01:20.662
take the two from the left.

00:01:20.662 --> 00:01:23.880
Four against two: take the right two.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.241
Four against three: take three.

00:01:27.241 --> 00:01:30.150
Four against six: take four.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.060
Five against six: take five.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.970
Seven against six: take six.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.634
Now the right pile is empty

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:42.170
so seven is copied without a comparison.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.188
Eight elements, seven comparisons.

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.430
Each step moves one card

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.052
so merging n cards takes at most n steps

00:01:51.052 --> 00:01:53.380
merge runs in linear time.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.562
And look at the two twos.

00:01:55.562 --> 00:01:58.128
When the front elements are equal

00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:00.253
less than or equal takes the

00:02:00.326 --> 00:02:02.085
one from the left first.

00:02:02.085 --> 00:02:04.724
So merge sort, like insertion sort

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:05.530
is stable.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.143
In Java, merge first copies the two runs

00:02:10.244 --> 00:02:14.076
into temporary arrays, left and right.

00:02:14.076 --> 00:02:18.210
Index i walks along left, j along right

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.630
and k along the output.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.104
While both runs have elements

00:02:23.104 --> 00:02:25.653
it takes the smaller front element

00:02:25.724 --> 00:02:27.423
less than or equal keeps

00:02:27.494 --> 00:02:28.910
equal keys in order.

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:31.672
Two short loops after it copy whatever

00:02:31.742 --> 00:02:33.300
is left of either run.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.798
Merge sort itself is short.

00:02:35.798 --> 00:02:37.947
It finds the middle point

00:02:37.947 --> 00:02:41.050
sorts the two halves, and merges them.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.458
The middle is computed as

00:02:45.458 --> 00:02:46.380
l + (r - l) / 2.

00:02:46.473 --> 00:02:48.594
It gives the same value

00:02:48.686 --> 00:02:50.623
as (l + r) / 2

00:02:50.623 --> 00:02:53.113
but for very large arrays

00:02:53.113 --> 00:02:55.880
l + r can overflow an int.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:57.190
Two pitfalls.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.183
Forget the leftover loops

00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:02.061
and the rest of the other run is lost.

00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:05.161
And do not merge in place by overwriting

00:03:05.161 --> 00:03:07.892
that destroys values not yet merged.

00:03:07.892 --> 00:03:10.919
That is why merge copies the runs first

00:03:10.993 --> 00:03:13.355
so merge sort needs extra memory

00:03:13.428 --> 00:03:14.757
proportional to n.

00:03:14.757 --> 00:03:16.750
Insertion sort needs none.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.736
Here is the whole algorithm

00:03:19.826 --> 00:03:22.588
on seven numbers: 38

00:03:22.588 --> 00:03:26.153
27, 43, 3, 9

00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:27.669
82, 10.

00:03:27.669 --> 00:03:30.877
The middle of 0 to 6 is 3

00:03:30.966 --> 00:03:34.086
so the left half gets four elements

00:03:34.175 --> 00:03:36.403
and the right half three.

00:03:36.403 --> 00:03:38.542
The halves split again

00:03:38.542 --> 00:03:40.770
down to single elements.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.629
How many calls does merge sort

00:03:43.713 --> 00:03:46.138
make on these seven elements?

00:03:46.138 --> 00:03:48.898
And how many of them call merge?

00:03:48.898 --> 00:03:50.320
Pause and count.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.078
Then the merges

00:03:55.078 --> 00:03:57.582
from the smallest pieces upwards

00:03:57.582 --> 00:03:59.644
27, 38.

00:03:59.644 --> 00:04:01.117
3, 43.

00:04:01.117 --> 00:04:04.063
Then 3, 27, 38

00:04:04.063 --> 00:04:05.020
43.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.608
On the right: 9, 82.

00:04:08.608 --> 00:04:11.534
Then 9, 10, 82.

00:04:11.534 --> 00:04:16.130
And the final merge gives the sorted array.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.722
Every call on two or more elements

00:04:19.817 --> 00:04:21.343
makes two calls

00:04:21.343 --> 00:04:25.347
and every call on one element makes none.

00:04:25.443 --> 00:04:27.922
For n elements, that is

00:04:28.017 --> 00:04:31.640
2n - 1 calls: 13 for 7.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.037
The seven calls on single

00:04:34.118 --> 00:04:36.083
elements are base cases

00:04:36.083 --> 00:04:39.030
the other six each call merge once.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.010
How long does merge sort take?

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:44.410
Dividing takes constant time

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.730
conquering sorts two halves

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:50.090
and merging n elements takes linear time.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.890
Counting the comparisons in merge

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.610
the slides write this recurrence.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.124
Draw the recursion tree

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:00.195
for n a power of two.

00:05:00.195 --> 00:05:02.830
The root merges n elements.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:06.594
Its two children merge n / 2 each

00:05:06.594 --> 00:05:07.723
n in total.

00:05:07.817 --> 00:05:10.452
The four grandchildren merge

00:05:10.546 --> 00:05:13.840
n / 4 each: n in total again.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:18.267
Every level costs n, and cutting n in half

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:22.438
again and again, until it reaches one takes

00:05:22.533 --> 00:05:24.429
log_2 n levels.

00:05:24.429 --> 00:05:28.601
So T(n) is n log_2 n for powers of two

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:31.350
and O(n log n) in general.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.734
This program computes

00:05:33.812 --> 00:05:35.533
T(n) from the recurrence.

00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:37.645
For seven it gives twenty

00:05:37.724 --> 00:05:39.914
against 19.7

00:05:39.992 --> 00:05:41.244
for n log_2 n

00:05:41.244 --> 00:05:44.060
and for eight both are 24.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.957
For powers of two, the columns agree exactly

00:05:47.957 --> 00:05:50.295
for other n they stay close.

00:05:50.376 --> 00:05:52.794
Lecture six solves recurrences

00:05:52.875 --> 00:05:54.890
like this one in general.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.374
Merge sort's O(n log n) grows much more

00:05:58.445 --> 00:06:01.207
slowly than insertion sort's n^2.

00:06:01.278 --> 00:06:04.252
This program counts the key comparisons of

00:06:04.323 --> 00:06:07.015
both on the same pseudo-random arrays.

00:06:07.085 --> 00:06:09.210
For eight and sixteen elements

00:06:09.281 --> 00:06:10.910
the counts are similar.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.378
But at 100,000

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.230
insertion sort makes about 2.5

00:06:16.311 --> 00:06:18.511
billion comparisons

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:22.177
and merge sort about 1.5 million

00:06:22.177 --> 00:06:24.540
over 1000 times fewer.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.222
The O hides constants, though.

00:06:27.299 --> 00:06:29.087
Merge sort pays for its

00:06:29.165 --> 00:06:30.874
calls and its copying

00:06:30.952 --> 00:06:32.973
while insertion sort has a

00:06:33.051 --> 00:06:34.760
very tight inner loop.

00:06:34.838 --> 00:06:36.703
So insertion sort may be

00:06:36.781 --> 00:06:38.569
faster on small inputs

00:06:38.646 --> 00:06:40.745
and for large enough inputs

00:06:40.823 --> 00:06:42.610
merge sort always wins.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.174
Use each where it is strong.

00:06:45.174 --> 00:06:47.625
A hybrid sort runs merge sort

00:06:47.625 --> 00:06:51.104
but sorts small pieces with insertion sort.

00:06:51.104 --> 00:06:53.951
Java's Arrays.sort for objects

00:06:53.951 --> 00:06:56.560
and Python's sort, both do this.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.409
Finally, a first look at quicksort

00:06:59.409 --> 00:07:01.549
another divide-and-conquer sort

00:07:01.549 --> 00:07:03.818
lecture eleven covers it in depth.

00:07:03.818 --> 00:07:05.763
Pick an element as the pivot.

00:07:05.828 --> 00:07:08.226
Partition the array into the elements

00:07:08.291 --> 00:07:10.366
less than or equal to the pivot

00:07:10.366 --> 00:07:11.533
the pivot itself

00:07:11.533 --> 00:07:13.737
and the elements greater than it.

00:07:13.737 --> 00:07:16.460
Then quicksort the two parts recursively.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:18.730
There is no combine step

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.880
once both parts are sorted

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.953
the whole array is sorted.

00:07:22.953 --> 00:07:26.101
Merge sort does its work when combining

00:07:26.101 --> 00:07:29.095
quicksort does its work when dividing

00:07:29.095 --> 00:07:30.170
in partition.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.121
This partition, due to Lomuto

00:07:33.121 --> 00:07:35.967
uses the last element as the pivot

00:07:35.967 --> 00:07:36.836
here four.

00:07:36.915 --> 00:07:39.287
i marks the last element known

00:07:39.366 --> 00:07:41.263
to be at most the pivot

00:07:41.263 --> 00:07:43.950
and starts just before the array.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.074
Two is small: i moves to zero

00:07:47.074 --> 00:07:49.570
and two swaps with itself.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.731
Eight is large: the large region just grows.

00:07:53.731 --> 00:07:55.580
Seven is large too.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.291
One is small: i moves to one

00:07:58.291 --> 00:08:00.498
and one swaps with eight.

00:08:00.498 --> 00:08:03.214
Three is small: i moves to two

00:08:03.214 --> 00:08:05.590
and three swaps with seven.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.560
Five is large, and six is large.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.934
Finally the pivot swaps into the gap

00:08:12.934 --> 00:08:16.733
at index i + 1, three: its final place.

00:08:16.733 --> 00:08:20.448
Two, one, three are to its left, and seven

00:08:20.448 --> 00:08:23.065
five, six, eight to its right

00:08:23.065 --> 00:08:24.415
not yet sorted.

00:08:24.415 --> 00:08:28.130
That is the job of the two recursive calls.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.429
Quicksort's average time is O(n log n).

00:08:31.429 --> 00:08:34.162
But with an unlucky pivot every time

00:08:34.234 --> 00:08:36.752
for example an already sorted array

00:08:36.824 --> 00:08:39.054
with the last element as pivot

00:08:39.125 --> 00:08:41.355
it makes n levels of partitions

00:08:41.427 --> 00:08:44.520
instead of log n, and takes quadratic time.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.028
To sum up the lecture.

00:08:47.028 --> 00:08:50.882
One: insertion sort is in place and stable

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.124
linear on sorted input and quadratic

00:08:54.212 --> 00:08:55.789
in the worst case.

00:08:55.789 --> 00:08:58.592
Two: merge sort splits in half

00:08:58.592 --> 00:09:02.535
sorts both halves and merges in linear time

00:09:02.535 --> 00:09:04.112
for O(n log n)

00:09:04.112 --> 00:09:06.740
at the price of extra memory.

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:10.869
Three: quicksort partitions around a pivot

00:09:10.869 --> 00:09:12.890
then sorts both sides.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.321
Try this yourself

00:09:15.436 --> 00:09:20.060
draw the divide and merge steps of merge

00:09:20.176 --> 00:09:24.568
sort on the letters M E R G E S O R T.

00:09:24.568 --> 00:09:27.920
How many calls does it make?

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:31.596
Next lecture: asymptotic notation, big O

00:09:31.596 --> 00:09:33.623
big Omega and big Theta

00:09:33.623 --> 00:09:37.030
the language for all these running times.

